  "body": "## Change and mathematical scope\n\nThis is a **construction and scheduling tool** for dirty fanout. Sharing a temporary row across output updates saves restoration work, but can leave several outputs partly updated. This scheduler caps the number of unfinished outputs. Once an output is committed, it is correct and is never touched again.\n\nFor five outputs with a cap of two:\n\n| Schedule | Most outputs unfinished at once | Child calls |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| This construction | 2 | 404 |\n| Unrestricted optimum | 3 | 300 |\n| Compute and restore each output separately | 1 | 716 |\n\nAll three use four dirty rows. The 716-call baseline is separate restoration, not the optimal cap-one schedule, which costs 508 calls. These are exact operation counts, not measured speedups or memory savings.\n\nFor $k$ outputs and capacity $W$, the minimum query cost is\n\n$$\nQ_{\\min}=2\\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}c_{A_i}+2\\left\\lceil\\frac{k}{2W}\\right\\rceil c_B.\n$$\n\nHere $c_{A_i}$ prices one complete output-readout call and $c_B$ one complete shared-source call. Prices must be positive, fixed and equal for a call and its inverse. The written lower bound covers arbitrary extra readouts in the declared query alphabet. It does not establish optimality over arbitrary circuits.\n\nThe [construction and paid recurrence](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/blob/b53b07b5aed4e00358a0d0061ad99dbf5190ca2b/research/bounded-pending-fanout-seed53/README.md#complete-recurrence-and-fixture) also charge admission, preparation, control, source access and child restoration. Supplier placement, common-label realization, precision/tape integration and outer assembly remain open. **No new $\\kappa$ is claimed.**\n\n## Task and overlap\n\nScope key: `dirty-fanout/pending-cap/permanent-commit`. This follows the user's recursive-interface assignment, seed 53. The domain is $k\\geq0$, $W\\geq1$, with exact characteristic-zero linear maps, signed unit queries and arbitrary initial data. The [task card](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/blob/b53b07b5aed4e00358a0d0061ad99dbf5190ca2b/research/bounded-pending-fanout-seed53/CLAIM.md) records the bounded experiment and stopped earlier candidate.\n\nInvestigation base: `ce7ad1b6ab09452c3478723a2e96ed5ff212dc08`. Upstream advanced during the cycle; this remains the base used for the result.\n\n| Predecessor | Exact dependency head | Result used |\n|---|---|---|\n| @CubeJerry, #889 | `e2021d1c9e06948528cbc0a8fd7079b671da981d` | Dirty fanout and its unrestricted weighted minimum |\n| Ross Richardson, #938 | `df645942469e6a739ec94010a6068c398f4a6c86` | Coefficient states and all unrestricted minimum words |\n\nThe addition is the capacity-constrained scheduler, permanent-commit checker and lower bound for longer words below the unrestricted schedule's required capacity. #982 and #1103 instead borrow output rows; this construction reserves them throughout.\n\n## Validation\n\nRun from the repository root with standard-library Python 3.11 or later:\n\n```sh\npython3 -I -B research/bounded-pending-fanout-seed53/verify.py\npython3 -I -O -B research/bounded-pending-fanout-seed53/test_package.py\n```\n\nExpected results are `PASS_BOUNDED_PENDING_FANOUT` and `PASS_BOUNDED_PENDING_PACKAGE`. Both pass on macOS arm64 with CPython 3.11.15 and 3.14.8. The checks cover 529 capacity cases, exact dirty-state replay, every child return and five independently computed finite minima. A reordered word passes final-output checks but fails the child-restoration check. Actual file corruptions are rejected, and certificate regeneration is byte-identical.\n\nThe all-$k,W$ claim rests on the written proof. The tests provide finite evidence. [The validation record](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/blob/b53b07b5aed4e00358a0d0061ad99dbf5190ca2b/research/bounded-pending-fanout-seed53/VALIDATION.md) gives commands, hashes and controls. Selected-record integrity and its 25 existing tests pass. Full repository/cold supplier replay, Linux execution and formalization were not run; hosted CI success is not claimed.\n\n## Credit\n\nPrepared for Sagar Thakrar / @thakrarsagar with substantial OpenAI Codex assistance. @CubeJerry and Ross Richardson retain credit for the predecessor results above, including their attribution to Chafik Boukhalfa's #628 commutator work. Original source and notice files are preserved byte for byte. New work is Apache-2.0; [NOTICE.md](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/blob/b53b07b5aed4e00358a0d0061ad99dbf5190ca2b/research/bounded-pending-fanout-seed53/NOTICE.md) records the full attribution.\n",
